passing hobby like CB radio. I'll also note that some predictions of massive failure referred to specific technologies or business models, like the dot-com bubble, not the Internet itself. I'll structure the answer: Intro debunking "everyone", then reasons for skepticism, famous quotes, and then concluding that they underestimated human adaptability and innovation. Keep it informative and balanced.
00:01:17 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: That’s an interesting question—and it’s worth clearing up a common exaggeration first. Not *everyone* in the 1990s thought the Internet was a fad. Many researchers, tech enthusiasts, and early adopters were deeply excited about it. But there was a very vocal and influential group of skeptics—journalists, economists, business leaders—who publicly predicted the Internet would remain a niche hobby, fail commercially, or even collapse. Their doubts were rooted in the real limitations of the early Internet and in how hard it was to imagine a radically new way of living. Here’s why so many people (not everyone) thought the Internet was headed for failure or just a passing craze: ### 1. The Internet looked like a clunky, academic toy In the early‑to‑mid 1990s, before the Web went mainstream, going online meant text‑only interfaces, cryptic commands, screeching modems, and slow speeds (often 14.4 or 28.8 kbps). It was mostly used by universities, government researchers, and hobbyists. To a typical consumer or business executive, it seemed utterly impractical for daily life. The idea that ordinary people would buy groceries, watch movies, or do their banking this way seemed absurd. ### 2. A terrible user experience (by today’s standards) Before graphical browsers like Mosaic (1993) and Netscape (1994) took off, the Internet simply wasn’t friendly. Even after the Web arrived, pages loaded at a crawl, connections dropped constantly, and search engines were primitive. Critics argued that newspapers, physical stores, and telephones were vastly more convenient—why would anyone tolerate this frustrating technology? ### 3. The “no one will ever buy online” argument A huge wall of skepticism centered on commerce. Many experts were sure consumers would never trust the Internet with their credit card numbers. Widely quoted lines from the time include: - **“The Internet will never catch on for e‑commerce because people are afraid to give out their credit card number.”** (A summary of a common 1994‑95 sentiment.) - Newsweek’s 1995 article **“The Internet? Bah!”** by astronomer and writer Clifford Stoll declared: “No online database will replace your daily newspaper… no computer network will change the way government works.” He also dismissed the idea of buying books or cars online, calling it a “hype” that ignored the physical realities of shopping. ### 4. Predictions of technical collapse Some predictions weren’t about adoption but about infrastructure. Ethernet co‑inventor Robert Metcalfe famously wrote in a 1995 InfoWorld column that the Internet would “go spectacularly supernova and in 1996 catastrophically collapse.” He was so confident that he literally ate his words (blending a printed copy of his column with liquid and drinking it) on stage in 1997 after he was proven wrong. The concern was that exponential growth would overwhelm the network, leading to permanent congestion. ### 5. Comparisons to past fads Skeptics repeatedly compared the Internet to previous over‑hyped technologies that failed to change the world: CB radio, interactive television, or the videophone. In 1998, economist Paul Krugman (yes, the future Nobel laureate) said, “By 2005 or so, it will become clear that the Internet’s impact on the economy has been no greater than the fax machine’s.” He wasn’t saying it would vanish, but that its economic effect would be trivial—a sentiment many shared before e‑commerce exploded. ### 6. The dot‑com bust reinforced the “I told you so” When the dot‑com bubble burst in 2000–2001, many skeptics felt vindicated. Dozens of high‑profile Internet companies with sky‑high valuations (Pets.com, Webvan, eToys) went bankrupt. Critics used this to argue that the Internet’s commercial promise was a mirage. What they missed was the difference between unsustainable business models and the underlying technology, which continued to grow. By the time companies like Amazon and Google proved the skeptics wrong on a massive scale, the “fad” narrative had quietly died. ### Why the skeptics were wrong, and why it matters The common thread in most of these doubts was a failure of imagination: people judged the future Internet by the flawed, slow, inconvenient Internet of the early 1990s. They didn’t foresee broadband, Wi‑Fi, smartphones, social media, or how deeply the Internet would embed itself into every aspect of life. The famous predictions of failure are now a classic cautionary tale about underestimating exponential technological change.
